{"id":"fraud-unit-by-state","title":"California's Medicaid fraud unit against the other largest States","question":"How many cases, convictions and dollars does California's Medicaid fraud control unit report each year, and how does that compare with the other largest programs?","finding":"California's unit reported 186 convictions in fiscal 2018 and 43 in fiscal 2025, while the State's Medicaid spending went from $88,895.8 million to $173,402.7 million and the unit's staff from 205 to 290. That is 2.5 convictions for every $10 billion of Medicaid spending in 2025 (20.9 in 2018), against a middle figure of 20.8 for the five other largest programs (New York, Texas, Pennsylvania, Florida, Ohio); California ranks 49 of 53 units on that measure. It reported $102.1 million in recoveries in 2025, 1.4 times what the unit cost.","how":"From the statistical charts the federal inspector general publishes each year for the State Medicaid fraud control units, fiscal 2018 to 2025, as reported by the units. California is shown for every year, and the five other States with the largest Medicaid spending in the latest year for the first and latest years. Convictions per $10 billion divides total convictions by total Medicaid spending in the same chart; recoveries per unit dollar divides total recoveries by the unit's own spending.","limits":"The inspector general notes that recoveries are amounts defendants are required to pay and \"may not reflect actual collections\"; one large case can dominate a year, as the swing in California's own recoveries from one year to the next shows. A conviction counted this year can come from an investigation opened years earlier. Cases brought by other agencies, such as the federal prosecutions on the enforcement page, are not in these counts, and the charts do not say how each State divides the work. A low count is a question about capacity and choices, not evidence about how much fraud exists.","tier":"OFFICIAL","columns":[{"k":"state","t":"State","type":"text"},{"k":"fiscalYear","t":"Fiscal year","type":"year"},{"k":"investigations","t":"Open investigations","type":"int"},{"k":"indictments","t":"Indictments","type":"int"},{"k":"convictions","t":"Convictions","type":"int"},{"k":"fraudConvictions","t":"Of which fraud","type":"int"},{"k":"recoveries","t":"Recoveries","type":"usd"},{"k":"unitSpending","t":"Unit spending","type":"usd"},{"k":"medicaid","t":"Medicaid spending","type":"usd"},{"k":"staff","t":"Staff","type":"int"},{"k":"perTen","t":"Convictions per $10 billion of Medicaid","type":"int"},{"k":"peersPerTen","t":"Same, middle of five other largest States","type":"int"},{"k":"back","t":"Recoveries per unit dollar","type":"times"},{"k":"peersBack","t":"Same, five other largest States","type":"times"}],"rows":[{"state":"California","fiscalYear":2025,"investigations":1052,"indictments":83,"convictions":43,"fraudConvictions":33,"recoveries":102126213,"unitSpending":74421308,"medicaid":173402740079,"staff":290,"perTen":2.5,"peersPerTen":20.8,"back":1.4,"peersBack":3.2},{"state":"California","fiscalYear":2024,"investigations":1089,"indictments":52,"convictions":48,"fraudConvictions":40,"recoveries":583830457,"unitSpending":69208433,"medicaid":157098115172,"staff":270,"perTen":3.1,"peersPerTen":16.2,"back":8.4,"peersBack":1.1},{"state":"California","fiscalYear":2023,"investigations":1136,"indictments":49,"convictions":67,"fraudConvictions":56,"recoveries":362823882,"unitSpending":66345930,"medicaid":130133324961,"staff":236,"perTen":5.1,"peersPerTen":13.2,"back":5.5,"peersBack":1.4},{"state":"California","fiscalYear":2022,"investigations":1327,"indictments":66,"convictions":66,"fraudConvictions":42,"recoveries":108517301,"unitSpending":54820183,"medicaid":125017780154,"staff":220,"perTen":5.3,"peersPerTen":19.8,"back":2.0,"peersBack":3.0},{"state":"California","fiscalYear":2021,"investigations":1472,"indictments":65,"convictions":88,"fraudConvictions":53,"recoveries":72973624,"unitSpending":44307262,"medicaid